  "interpretation": [
    "The one-measurement shortcuts of the four hand-designed topology archetypes do not generalize to the 12-world random panels; typical exact target resolution requires three relational measurements.",
    "Nevertheless, a very small relational subset resolves topology-sensitive targets despite complete static nodewise equivalence across all 12 worlds.",
    "Full topology identity itself is recoverable with only three or four REL/FP/KO measurements and no raw-edge query in every row.",
    "Intervention response is slightly coarser than complete pairwise relation structure, requiring fewer measurements on average.",
    "Cross-family exact minima are common, so the most useful measurement family need not match the semantic target family.",
    "No single relational measurement is universally first; evidence design is world-set and target dependent."
  ],
  "claim_boundary": [
    "Measurement counts are conceptual information counts, not field costs.",
    "The 12-world panels are controlled DAG ensembles rather than empirical dispersal networks.",
    "Full topology recovery from a few relational measurements is conditional on this finite candidate universe and does not imply that arbitrary real networks are identifiable from three observations.",
    "The next valid question is adaptive evidence design: whether outcome-dependent sequential measurement can reduce expected or worst-case burden relative to one fixed nonadaptive panel."
  ]
